## 4. Bhagat Singh and Gandhi, held together

"I am Bhagat Singh and I am Gandhi." In 1931 the two methods were rivals. Gandhi's truth-force (satyagraha) was strict nonviolence. Bhagat Singh's organisation, the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association, had killed a policeman. Yet the acts this document follows are the ones the two share:
- speech made public at personal cost;
- arrest accepted, not evaded;
- the court used as a platform;
- the fast as the last means of speech;
- truth as the only weapon that is never taken away.

The court statement draws that boundary inside Bhagat Singh's own record ("It is not the cult of the bomb and the pistol"), and the workflow's means are on that side of it:
- the record;
- the warrant;
- the court;
- the vote;
- the public web;
- the work given away as a commons (hyperactive-labs-nonprofit).

Holding both names as one standpoint is many-sidedness, the doctrine report-one-direction-of-error states as "The form that fixes it", and the one Avik named for Russell: "anekantevad. fuck. that's all I ever had to say."

The atheism is the same move once more. "It is my mode of thinking that has made me an atheist" is a claim settled by reasoning in the open, against the charge that it is vanity. Avik's version is "I only know what's true". His dharma document, docs/hyperactivity-is-the-dharma.md, treats identity as a path, not a boundary. On that reading, Bhagat Singh is one path from the Lahore of 1928 to the knowledge graph of 2026: the same demand, carried forward by other means.

